561	// a record reaching around it to name its own was a second authority over the same
562	// fact. What a record legitimately knows is narrower and is all `applyStage` asks
563	// for: whether its change needs a session at all, and what that session must do.
564	//
565	// The field is not merely ignored, it is REJECTED. An ignored field on a record
566	// someone writes in good faith is work that silently never happens; the whole reason
567	// the note existed was that skipping such work quietly is a correctness risk (#405).
568	// Failing at load turns a stale or hopeful note into an error with a fix attached.
569	export function assertNoAgenticNote(m) {
570	  if (m?.agentic === undefined || m?.agentic === null) return;
571	  throw new Error(`migration ${m.id}: the "agentic" field was retired in #768 Phase 5 and is no longer run. `
572	    + 'Declare `applyStage: { why, instructions }` instead — same intent, without the model knob, which the '
573	    + 'scheduler owns. Engine records may not carry it at all (DESIGN §5); a pack record may.');
574	}
575	
576	// THE `applyStage` FIELD: this record's change needs a session on the member.
577	//
578	// It exists because the alternative was worse. The pack flow used to decide the
579	// apply stage from its VERSION PLAN — "any pack version moved" — which is a fact
580	// about the CANON, while the stage exists for a fact about the MEMBER: the pack's
581	// new rules met content the canon has never seen. Since a record can only reach an
582	// up-to-date member if its pack's manifest version bumps, and every bump fired the
583	// stage, a purely mechanical migration — a regex rewrite, a declaration seed — could
584	// not be shipped without spending an agent session on every member in the fleet
585	// (#798). The record's author knows which of the two kinds they wrote; a version
586	// number never can.
587	//
588	// SHAPE, VALIDATED AT LOAD for the same reason the retired field is rejected there:
589	// a record whose declaration is malformed must fail where it is written, not go
590	// quietly unread by the flow that would have acted on it.
591	//
592	//   - `why` (required, non-empty string) — what the session is for, in the PR and in
593	//     the log. The terminal vocabulary insists every non-green end be explainable
594	//     (the update flows' terminal vocabulary), and this is the sentence for this one.
595	//   - `instructions` (optional string) — appended to the standing brief. The standing
596	//     brief is policy that holds for every apply stage; this is what only this record
597	//     knows, and without it the declaration would be a bare boolean that tells the
598	//     session nothing about the change that summoned it.
599	//
600	// ENGINE RECORDS MAY NOT CARRY IT. "No agentic work in the engine flow. Ever."
601	// (DESIGN §5) — and the flow is read off where the record LIVES, so this is checked
602	// against the same structural classifier and cannot be misdeclared.
603	export function assertApplyStageDeclaration(m) {
604	  const declared = m?.applyStage;
605	  if (declared === undefined || declared === null) return;
606	  const bad = (what) => { throw new Error(`migration ${m.id}: ${what}`); };
607	  if (m.dir && flowOf(m.dir).flow !== 'pack') {
608	    bad('only a PACK record may declare "applyStage" — the engine update flow has no agentic lane (DESIGN §5). '
609	      + 'If the change needs a session on each member, it belongs to the pack whose rules it changes.');
610	  }
611	  if (typeof declared !== 'object' || Array.isArray(declared)) {
612	    bad('"applyStage" must be an object `{ why, instructions }`, not a bare flag — a stage nobody can explain '
613	      + 'is one nobody will trust the next time it fires.');
614	  }
615	  if (typeof declared.why !== 'string' || declared.why.trim() === '') {
616	    bad('"applyStage.why" must be a non-empty string saying what the session is for; it is what the PR and the log report.');
617	  }
618	  if (declared.instructions !== undefined && typeof declared.instructions !== 'string') {
619	    bad('"applyStage.instructions" must be a string — it is appended verbatim to the apply-stage brief.');
620	  }
621	}
622	
